potential with the inflaton initiallystarting at some large amplitude.","filename":"potential.png"},{"key":"74143b13e55d88496c07ef176e0c3305","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/74143b13e55d88496c07ef176e0c3305","source":"arxiv","caption":"Contributions to the $\\phi$ self-energy of order $g^2$ (left) and$g^4$ (right).","filename":"simple.png"},{"key":"cf135d35b8e94b832dc54c7a2dfe9f62","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/cf135d35b8e94b832dc54c7a2dfe9f62","source":"arxiv","caption":"The different approximations for the friction coefficient are shown in the intermediate and low temperature region. The full expression, plotted on the left, corresponds to Eq. (\\ref{simplegam}) and the low temperature approximation to Eq. (\\ref{flt}). These plots include both $\\phi\\to\\chi\\to2\\sigma$ and $\\phi\\to\\sigma\\chi$decay channels. Coupling constants are $h^2/8\\pi=0.025$ and $m_\\chi=m$.","filename":"smallh.png"},{"key":"c0665cde982c0cbeb2dae843c6fbfca6","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/c0665cde982c0cbeb2dae843c6fbfca6","source":"arxiv","caption":"The different approximations for the friction coefficient are shown in the intermediate and low temperature region. The full expression, plotted on the left, corresponds to Eq. (\\ref{simplegam}) and the low temperature approximation to Eq. (\\ref{flt}). These plots include both $\\phi\\to\\chi\\to2\\sigma$ and $\\phi\\to\\sigma\\chi$decay channels. Coupling constants are $h^2/8\\pi=0.025$ and $m_\\chi=m$.","filename":"lowT.png"},{"key":"fe37019e31c30069e1d903cce9612795","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fe37019e31c30069e1d903cce9612795","source":"arxiv","caption":"This plot shows the momentum dependence of the particle production rate $S_p$ for the production of low mass fields through an intermediate heavy field. Thethermal distribution $n$ is shown for comparison.","filename":"Sp.png"},{"key":"fbea8a282392d50e2336492bd9319880","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/fbea8a282392d50e2336492bd9319880","source":"arxiv","caption":"Warm inflation for hybrid quadratic potential: Evolution of the ratios $Q$ (top lines), $T/H$ (middle lines) and $T/\\phi$ (bottom lines) depending on the number of e-folds to the end of inflation, for different values of ${\\cal N}$=10000, 20000, 30000, with $g_*=228.75$,$\\phi(0)/m_P=0.21$, $\\eta =3$, and $V_0^{1/4}/m_P=3\\times 10^{-4}$.","filename":"fighybqp.png"},{"key":"42c43f2476b5bb8c4af5299bb748c108","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/42c43f2476b5bb8c4af5299bb748c108","source":"arxiv","caption":"Hybrid quadratic potential: Spectral index depending on the no. of e-folds to the end of inflation, for different values of ${\\cal N}$=10000,20000,30000, with $g_*=228.75$, $\\phi(0)/m_P=0.21$, $\\eta=3$,and $V_0^{1/4}/m_P=3\\times 10^{-4}$.","filename":"fig2hybqp.png"},{"key":"ecfd6515a61b35a9a37d63604aba7d47","url":"https://inspirehep.net/files/ecfd6515a61b35a9a37d63604aba7d47","source":"arxiv","caption":"The figure shows the $\\log C_\\phi$-$\\log |\\eta|$ plane, where $C_{\\phi} \\equiv 0.64 h^4 N_{\\chi} N^2_{\\rm decay}$. The light grey (light green) area represents the space where the thermal spectrum of perturbations matches the observed amplitude $N_*\\sim50$ $e$-foldings before the end of inflation, with \\mbox{$\\phi_{\\rm end}<10\\,m_P$}. Within the dark grey (reddish) band, the resulting value of the spectral index is within the 1-$\\sigma$ window: \\mbox{$n=0.960^{+0.014}_{-0.013}$}, as inferred by WMAP+BAO+SN data in the $\\Lambda$CDM model for negligible tensor perturbations. In the graph lines of constant $H$ and\\mbox{$Q\\equiv\\Upsilon/3H$} are also depicted.","filename":"fighilltop.png"}],"texkeys":["Berera:2008ar"],"citeable":true,"keywords":[{"value":"12.60.Jv","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"98.80.Cq","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"11.10.Wx","schema":"PACS"},{"value":"cosmology","source":"author"},{"value":"inflation","source":"author"},{"value":"dissipation","source":"author"},{"value":"particle production","source":"author"},{"value":"supersymmetry","source":"author"},{"value":"inflation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"fluctuation: stochastic","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"field theory: finite temperature","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"dissipation","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"supersymmetry","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"potential","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"particle: production","schema":"INSPIRE"},{"value":"numerical calculations","schema":"INSPIRE"}],"refereed":true,"abstracts":[{"value":"The microscopic quantum field theory origins of warm inflation dynamics are reviewed. The warm inflation scenario is first described along with its results, predictions and comparison with the standard cold inflation scenario. The basics of thermal field theory required in the study of warm inflation are discussed. Quantum field theory real time calculations at finite temperature are then presented and the derivation of dissipation and stochastic fluctuations are shown from a general perspective. Specific results are given of dissipation coefficients for a variety of quantum field theory interaction structures relevant to warm inflation, in a form that can readily be used by model builders. Different particle physics models realising warm inflation are presented along with their observational predictions.","source":"arXiv"}],"references":[{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/154280"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"347","page_start":"347","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.D","journal_volume":"23"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/168781"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"389","page_start":"389","journal_title":"Phys.Lett.B","journal_volume":"108"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/literature/176612"},"reference":{"publication_info":{"artid":"1220","page_start":"1220","journal_title":"Phys.Rev.Lett.","journal_volume":"48"}},"curated_relation":false},{"record":{"$ref":"https://inspirehep.net/api/